<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Cyra app privacy manifest (App target).

  Scope: what the app itself sends off the device today, judged from src/lib/api.js,
  src/App.jsx, src/lib/pulse.js, src/lib/wearables.js, src/lib/notifications.js and
  backend/src/modules (ai, pulse, integrations, oauth, push). Apple's definition of
  "collect": transmitting data off the device in a way that allows you and/or your
  third-party partners to access it for longer than needed to service the request in
  real time. Where a flow was borderline, it is declared rather than left out.

  NOT collected, so not declared:
  - Apple Health data read by CyraHealthPlugin. It is read and kept on this device only
    (Library/NoCloud, excluded from backup); the plugin makes no network calls.
  - Reminders. The phone build schedules local notifications on the device; the Web Push
    subscription in backend/src/modules/push is used only by the web build.
  - The encrypted backup: a file the user keeps, with a key only she holds; no server.
  - No analytics, advertising, crash-reporting or tracking SDK is in the app.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>NSPrivacyTracking</key>
	<false/>
	<key>NSPrivacyTrackingDomains</key>
	<array/>
	<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypes</key>
	<array>
		<!--
		  File timestamp APIs (FileAttributeKey.creationDate and .modificationDate).
		  Not called by the App target's own Swift (AppDelegate, SceneDelegate,
		  CyraViewController, CyraHealthPlugin use createDirectory, setAttributes with
		  protectionKey, URL resource values isExcludedFromBackup, fileProtection and
		  isRegularFile, and contentsOfDirectory; none of these is on Apple's required
		  reason list). They are read by the Capacitor Filesystem plugin's library
		  (ion-ios-filesystem IONFILEItemAttributeModel, behind Filesystem.stat), which
		  Swift Package Manager links statically into this App executable. Neither
		  CapacitorFilesystem 8.1.4 nor ion-ios-filesystem ships its own privacy
		  manifest, and Apple requires the bundle containing the executable to report the
		  API. C617.1: timestamps of files inside the app container only.
		  Remove this entry if a future Filesystem release bundles its own manifest.
		-->
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryFileTimestamp</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>
			<array>
				<string>C617.1</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
	</array>
	<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypes</key>
	<array>
		<!--
		  Health. Leaves the device in:
		  - POST /api/ai/welcome: life stage, age band, cycle length and regularity, goals.
		  - POST /api/ai/ask: the life-stage label (with the question, below).
		  - POST /api/pulse/tally (opt-in only): stage plus allowlisted symptom flags
		    (hot flashes, rough night, cramps, mood dip, heavy day, nausea, kicks, swelling).
		  When ANTHROPIC_API_KEY is set, the backend forwards the AI inputs to Anthropic,
		  a third-party processor, whose retention is longer than real time. Pulse
		  persists weekly counts.
		  Oura and Terra (Fitbit, Garmin, Whoop) rows reach the device through the backend:
		  Oura rows are relayed in real time; Terra rows (temperature, resting heart rate,
		  HRV, sleep) wait in server memory for up to 7 days, keyed by the Terra reference
		  id (backend/src/modules/integrations/index.js, inbox map).
		  Linked: that reference id is declared below as a linked User ID, because Terra
		  ties it to the person's wearable account. Health rows stored under it are
		  therefore linked too (Apple: data is unlinked only when direct identifiers such
		  as a user ID are stripped before collection). The AI and pulse flows above carry
		  no name, email, account, reference id or device identifier, but the label is per
		  data type, so Health as a whole is declared linked.
		  Product personalization: welcome writes a personal greeting. (Intake routing to
		  the single life-stage experience runs on the device and sends nothing.)
		  Depends on product behavior: if the Terra inbox goes away (or stops keying rows by
		  the reference id), Health could return to not linked.
		-->
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeHealth</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
			<true/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
			<array>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeProductPersonalization</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
		<!--
		  Sensitive info: Apple lists pregnancy information here. It is sent as the
		  "Pregnancy" or "Trying to Conceive" stage labels in /api/ai/welcome and /api/ai/ask, and the pregnancy pulse flags
		  (nausea, kicks, swelling). Same purposes as Health above.
		  Not linked: none of these requests carries a name, email, account, Terra
		  reference id or device identifier, and the backend keeps no user account. Pulse
		  sends a random token that changes every week; the server keeps only its hash, in
		  memory, to count each person once per week. The Terra inbox (the reason Health is
		  linked) holds wearable rows only, never pregnancy information.
		  Depends on product behavior: declare linked if any of these requests ever carries
		  an identifier.
		-->
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeSensitiveInfo</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
			<array>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeProductPersonalization</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
		<!--
		  Other user content: the free-text "Ask Cyra" question (up to 500 characters),
		  POST /api/ai/ask, forwarded to Anthropic when a key is set. Sent without a name or
		  identifier, but it can contain whatever the person types.
		-->
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeOtherUserContent</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
			<array>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
		<!--
		  Product interaction: the opt-in pulse flag "report" (opened the doctor report,
		  src/App.jsx contribute(["report"])), stored only as an aggregate weekly count and
		  shown back to users once at least k people share it.
		-->
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeProductInteraction</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
			<array>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
		<!--
		  Email address and name: optional social sign-in (Apple, Google, Facebook) through
		  /api/oauth. The backend receives the verified email, plus a name if the provider
		  sends one, and holds them in memory for at most 5 minutes under a one-time code
		  until this device collects them. No account record is kept. Declared because the
		  hand-off outlives a single request. They identify the person, so they are linked.
		  Anonymous Mode and email registration send neither.
		-->
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeEmailAddress</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
			<true/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
			<array>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeName</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
			<true/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
			<array>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
		<!--
		  User ID: the random Terra reference id the device creates when someone connects
		  Fitbit, Garmin or Whoop (src/lib/wearables.js newRef). It is sent to the backend
		  and passed to Terra as reference_id, and Terra keeps it with that person's wearable
		  connection, and the backend keys the queued Terra rows by it (see Health above).
		  Declared as linked because Terra, the aggregator, can tie it to the wearable
		  account.
		-->
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeUserID</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
			<true/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
			<array>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
		<!--
		  Other data types: Oura access and refresh tokens. After someone connects Oura, the
		  backend holds the tokens in memory for at most 5 minutes under a one-time code
		  until this device collects them (integrations/index.js, handoffs map); after
		  that, the device keeps them and sends them with each pull or refresh, which the
		  backend uses in real time only. Apple has no credentials category; Apple says a
		  token used on a server call and not retained need not be disclosed, but this
		  hand-off outlives a single request, the same reason Email and Name are declared.
		  They open the person's Oura account, so they are linked.
		  Depends on product behavior: if the hand-off stops holding tokens on the server,
		  this entry can go.
		-->
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeOtherDataTypes</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
			<true/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
			<array>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
	</array>
</dict>
</plist>
